## 2026-06-17 — Async cycle speedups: take the non-learning work off the cognitive lock

Goal: raise sustained cycle throughput by removing synchronous I/O and redundant CPU
from inside `async with self.lock` in the cognitive loop, WITHOUT touching the science.
The forward + backward + optimizer step, stage ordering, memory *reads*, and action
selection stay exactly where they are (no stale-weight overlap). Files backed up to
`.\backups\*.20260617_081700.bak` first (RULE #3).

Phase 0 — Measured first (RULE #6). GPU `full`+`hf`+discovered split (avg of 15 cycles):
encoders 31.5 ms, fwd 47.6 ms, bwd 30.2 ms, mem_recall 0.4 ms, stage10 0.15 ms, wall
103 ms (~9.7 Hz). Confirmed the ROI order: decode-once attacks the 31.5 ms encoders
bucket; the I/O offloads (logging, SQLite commits, trace writes) remove hot-path tax the
in-memory micro-benchmark doesn't even show.

Edits (surgical):
- `decadic/logging/json_logging.py` (+ `logging/__init__.py`): logging is now non-blocking.
  The root logger holds a single `QueueHandler`; a background `QueueListener` owns the
  real stream + rotating-file handlers (same JSON output + order). New `stop_logging()`;
  `decadic/api/app.py` lifespan stops the listener last on shutdown. Unconditional (pure
  win, no toggle).
- `decadic/nn/frozen_encoders.py`: decode-the-frame-ONCE. New `predecode(obs)` +
  `_decode_pixel_values`/`_decode_audio_features` produce CPU tensors a single time and
  stash them on `obs['_decoded']`; the pooled-vision, patch-token, and audio paths reuse
  them with one `.to(device)` (the two redundant CLIP preprocesses collapse to one).
  `decadic/agents/runtime.py` calls `predecode` in `handle_observation_dict` BEFORE the
  lock (no-op in zeros mode / stub cognition). Byte-identical output (GPU parity test:
  max|diff| = 0.0). Unconditional (pure win, no toggle).
- `decadic/memory/ltm_write_behind.py` (NEW): `WriteBehindLongTermGraph` mirrors the
  episodic write-behind for stage 10's WM→LTM consolidation. A single FIFO worker drains
  consolidation jobs off the cycle thread (graph's own RLock guards the in-memory mutate
  + commit); jobs snapshot the six slot fields `consolidate` reads at enqueue time so the
  worker never touches a since-mutated `MemorySlot`. Order-preserving sync fallback under
  backpressure; `flush()` before clear/backup/restore; drained on `stop()`. Always the
  graph the runtime constructs (like episodic) so the mode flips live. `config.py`
  `ltm_async_enabled()` (default ON, pinned OFF in conftest); `runtime.configure(ltm_async=…)`
  + `capacity_config()['ltm_async']`; `api/app.py` `configure_agent(ltm_async=…)`;
  `dashboard/src/api.ts` `CapacityConfig.ltm_async` + URL param; second checkbox in the
  "Performance (live)" group of `CognitionTogglesPanel.tsx`.
- `decadic/io/jsonl_writer.py` (NEW package `decadic/io`): a single daemon thread +
  queue that appends `(path, line)` items off-thread, with a write-lock so the worker
  and an inline backpressure write never race the same file (lost-line fix on Windows),
  `flush()`/`close()`, lazy/relazy worker start. The cycle-trace dump (`runtime.py`) and
  probe capture (`interpretability/probes.py`) now serialize on-cycle (content unchanged)
  and hand only the file append to it. Inherits existing gating (no new default-on flag).
- `tests/conftest.py`: pin `DECADIC_LTM_ASYNC=0` (CPU/zeros/fp32/synchronous baseline).

Decisions: new offloads default ON with live toggles (matching episodic-async); logging
+ decode-once are unconditional pure wins; the cognition trace defers only the file I/O
(the two frozen forward passes stay on-cycle, cheap on GPU). All deferred memory writes
carry a ~one-cycle visibility lag (immaterial — recall/re-id read the prior cycle), and
are flushed before backup/restore/clear.

Tests: 5 new files — `test_ltm_write_behind.py` (parity vs sync, no-loss reopen, clear
flush, order-preserving backpressure, born-disabled, live toggle), `test_jsonl_writer.py`
(flush/no-loss/byte-identical/backpressure/relazy), `test_logging_async.py` (root holds
only QueueHandler, records drain to file + flush on stop, idempotent re-init),
`test_predecode.py` (zeros no-op + GPU decode-once byte-identical parity), and a live
`test_configure_ltm_async_toggle_is_live` in `test_faculties.py`. Full suite: 488 passed,
7 skipped (pre-existing GPU/HF-gated unrelated). The JSONL backpressure test caught a
genuine concurrent-append data race, fixed with the writer's io-lock.

## 2026-06-17 — Hold movement mode (run the active stance until disabled)

Goal: a master "Hold movement" toggle so the operator can keep the active stance
/motion running indefinitely with the joint braces fully welded -- the ROM
curriculum suspended (no range-of-motion release) and motion stances looping
continuously -- until they turn it off. The training is working; this lets a
movement be driven precisely on repeat rather than gradually freeing the joints.
The root is never forced, so the no-glide invariant still holds.

Edits (surgical; originals backed up to `.\backups\hold_20260617_080642\`):
- `scripts/mujoco_decadic_adapter.py`: `_movement_hold` flag + `set_movement_hold()`;
  new `_hold_braces()` (pins every `_tightness` to 1.0 + re-applies gains, leaving
  the PE EMA/dwell untouched so releasing hold resumes the ratchet); `step()` braced
  branch picks `_hold_braces()` vs `_update_braces()`; `_advance_motion()` loops when
  held (`stance.loop or self._movement_hold`) so the one-shot Rise also repeats;
  `movement_hold` on `BodySnapshot` + obs JSON; WS `hold_on`/`hold_off` dispatch.
- `decadic/api/app.py`: `POST /agent/{id}/body/movement_hold?enabled=bool`.
- `decadic/agents/runtime.py`: `movement_hold` in `_initial_metrics` + mirrored in
  `_capture_locomotion_telemetry` (telemetry only; inert to cognition).
- `dashboard/src/api.ts`: `movement_hold?` on `Metrics` + `setMovementHold()`.
  `dashboard/src/components/MotorPanel.tsx`: a "Hold movement" On/Off toggle row
  under the Stance selector (disabled when no agent or braces off).

Behavior notes: hold re-welds (the "Range of motion earned" readout shows 0% while
held); releasing resumes the ratchet from welded. Hold is global and persists
across stance switches. Only takes effect while the master joint braces are on.

Verification: full `pytest` 470 passed, 7 skipped (2 new hold tests: welded/no ROM
release under sustained low PE + resume on release; one-shot Rise loops while held);
dashboard `npm run build` (tsc+vite) OK.

## 2026-06-16 — Async episodic memory: live per-agent toggle, default ON

Goal: expose the write-behind episodic store (Phase 4 below) as a per-agent toggle in
the dashboard's Agent Settings tab, and make it ON by default (the user's call — keeps
the cognitive lock free; the synchronous write was a measured-but-now-removed stall).

Design: the write-behind store is now ALWAYS the store the runtime constructs, with a
live on/off switch and a LAZY worker — so when async is OFF it is byte-identical to a
bare EpisodicStore and spawns no thread (the test baseline, pinned OFF, has zero
overhead). The mode flips live because `append` (cycle) and `configure` (settings) both
run under the agent lock, so a flip can never race an in-flight write. Turning it OFF
drains the backlog and retires the worker; the store object is never swapped (no SQLite
connection migration), so the brain is never rebuilt.

Edits (originals backed up to `.\backups\*.20260616_235628.bak`):
- `decadic/config.py` — `episodic_async_enabled()` default flipped False -> True.
- `decadic/memory/write_behind.py` — rewrote as live-toggleable: `enabled` ctor arg,
  `set_async()` (lazy start / drain+stop), `async_enabled` property; `close()` now just
  `set_async(False)`. No-write-lost + flush-on-clear/backup/restore preserved.
- `decadic/agents/runtime.py` — always construct `WriteBehindEpisodicStore(..., enabled=
  episodic_async_enabled())`; `configure(episodic_async=...)` calls `set_async`;
  `capacity_config()` reports `episodic_async`.
- `decadic/api/app.py` — `configure_agent` accepts `episodic_async` and threads it through.
- `dashboard/src/api.ts` — `CapacityConfig.episodic_async` + `configureAgent` param.
- `dashboard/src/components/CognitionTogglesPanel.tsx` — "Performance (live)" group with
  the "Async episodic memory" checkbox (default on) in the selected-agent section.
