## 2026-06-16 — Fix: supine "back-glide" (heavier body + higher floor friction)

Symptom: the agent would glide horizontally across the floor on its back as a
quasi-rigid unit with seemingly static limbs, despite the torso never receiving
an external wrench. Root cause: light body (~42.8 kg) + low rolling/torsional
floor friction let the actuator/brace torque out-muscle the friction ceiling on
the rounded back capsules, so saturated motor commands acted as a steady
thruster. Per scope, the ROM brace logic/params and the brain objective
(neural_pipeline.py) were deliberately NOT touched (another dev owns the braces).

- scripts/mujoco_decadic_adapter.py: new BODY_MASS_KG constant (200 lb ~= 90.7 kg,
  env override DECADIC_BODY_MASS_KG). In HumanoidSim.__init__ the agent's kinematic
  subtree (bodies rooted at the torso free joint -- excludes the spliced NPC clone
  and scene props) is rescaled to that total: body_mass and body_inertia multiplied
  by the same factor, body_subtreemass[torso] set so _body_weight (and touch-load
  normalization) stay consistent. Measured: 42.8 kg (420 N) -> 90.7 kg (890 N).
- assets/humanoid_body.xml: floor geom friction "1 .1 .1" -> "1 .5 .5" (sliding
  unchanged; torsional/rolling 0.1 -> 0.5). condim=6 already engages these; the
  contact friction is the element-wise max of the two geoms, so the single floor
  edit covers every body-vs-floor contact.

